# v0.0.4.f1-alpha — unreleased candidate

**Release held: TRF-01 remains OPEN.** A new local interruption regression has
been repaired; its refined history proof and a fresh review ladder are pending.
No tag, push or GitHub release has been created. The earlier
[workflow record](../../closure/traffic-refinement/evidence/ting02-review-blocked/workflow.json)
and [final gate attempt](../../closure/traffic-refinement/evidence/ting02-review-blocked/final-gate-report.json)
preserve the previous candidate's incomplete reviews.

This maintenance release retains persistent JVM reuse and the existing analysis
algorithms while repairing configuration, HTTP decoding, deadline and connection
accounting defects found during production-to-Lean verification. Canonical stays
the default. Both metrics still compare against the complete correct pool,
including the oracle; behavioral scores, four instance categories, locators,
redacted edits, administrator uploads and Luna guidance keep their existing logic.

Raw request validation now rejects ambiguous separators, HTTP/0.9, missing or
conflicting HTTP/1.1 Host fields, invalid targets and repeated JSON charset
parameters before a business route runs. Header and body deadlines are checked
at completion, including buffered reads and JSON decoding. Public and loopback
control listeners retain independent admission budgets.

Three scheduling counterexamples showed two live request threads under a limit of
one. Reservations now survive handler return and are released only after a
zero-time join succeeds and the thread is no longer alive. Identification alone
is insufficient because it can precede startup publication. If a start attempt fails without
publishing an identity, its bounded slot remains occupied until restart; freeing
that slot earlier could admit another thread while the first is still starting.
Configuration guards also reject nonfinite, fractional or unsupported values
before allocating runtime resources and preserve accepted values exactly.

The [candidate report](../../closure/traffic-refinement/evidence/ting02-20261004T182729Z-cd397804/report.json)
records two identical clean offline Lean 4.34.1 builds, 303 theorem declarations
with empty transitive axiom sets, 52 registered correspondence mappings and 97
passing registered checks. That final six-review gate did not pass. The subsequent
local witness confirmed that an interrupted CPython liveness probe can report
termination while its native handler still runs. The current repair marks a probe
uncertain before either status call and permanently retains that slot if a call
fails. Ordinary successful observations still reclaim completed threads. The
new candidate must pass its own proof and review gate. The original obligation registry is
unchanged: TRF-00 is VERIFIED at its historical root, while TRF-01 and 21 later
obligations remain OPEN. The proof does not claim correctness of the OS, arbitrary
Python, external proxy normalization or future source revisions.

The [preserved regression evidence](../../closure/traffic-refinement/evidence/ting02-final-regressions/index.json)
records these local results:

- 1,047 Python regressions on the repaired production sources, with no input
  mutation during the run, and 378 Java engine checks.
- 77 portal browser scenarios and 7 dashboard checks in an isolated staged build.
- 23 cases in three arms (69 results): archived HTTP/projector behavior, current
  one-shot mode and current persistent mode. All compared semantic results
  matched, including both metrics, a nonprimary correct pool member, behavior,
  Unicode and invalid drafts, catalogue/detail routes, revalidation and reuse.
- A newly assembled private IIS package with all 181 exercises, 7,731 candidate
  predicates, seven dependency JARs, 178 class files and the new runtime modules.

This is finite local Linux evidence. Native Windows/macOS, a real IIS or
Cloudflare deployment, whole-process RSS and arbitrary execution histories are
outside this verification. Provider tests used disabled or mocked Luna; no paid
provider calls were required. Previously valid evidence and rejected candidates
are preserved with hashes.

The Git release tag is `v0.0.4.f1-alpha`; npm metadata uses the SemVer-compatible
`0.0.4-f1-alpha`. The preflight checks their exact documented correspondence.
Rebuild and install a timestamped IIS ZIP to update an existing deployment;
publishing a source release does not redeploy the website. Deployment ZIPs and
private configuration are not public release assets.
